---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What ReaderTweaks is, how the guides are tested, and how the site is funded — including advertising, affiliate links and review hardware."
  path="/about/"
  activeNav="about"
  jsonLd={breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about/' },
  ])}
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>ReaderTweaks</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A practical reference for getting more out of e-ink readers — organised by
        the exact device you own. It is an independent publication, and it is a
        commercial one: advertising, affiliate links and other partnerships help
        pay for it, but they do not buy our conclusions.
      </p>

      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        Every e-reader has a permanent hub here, and under each one the same seven
        sections: Guides, KOReader, Calibre, Customization, Troubleshooting,
        Accessories and Comparisons. The aim is that when you search for a
        specific problem on a specific device, the page you land on is written for
        that device and nothing else.
      </p>

      <h2 id="testing">How the guides are tested</h2>
      <p>
        Steps are walked through on the actual hardware and dated. E-reader
        firmware changes often, so when a step depends on a firmware version the
        page says which one. If something here is wrong or out of date, that is a
        bug — see contact below.
      </p>
      <p>
        The hardware we test on is a mix: devices we have bought, devices
        supplied or loaned by manufacturers or retailers, and accessories sent
        for evaluation. We do not commit to buying every device independently.
        What a supplied unit does not come with is a promise of coverage, a
        positive verdict, or any right to see a piece before it goes up. We write
        the same way whether we own the device or borrowed it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="funding">How ReaderTweaks is funded</h2>
      <p>
        ReaderTweaks is a commercial publication. The following pay, or may in
        future pay, for the work:
      </p>

      <h3>Affiliate links</h3>
      <p>
        Accessory links go to Amazon and carry our Amazon Associates tag. If you
        buy through one, we may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you.
        We recommend the accessory because it is the right one, and disclose the
        link because you should know it earns us something — those two facts sit
        together fine. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> covers the data
        side.
      </p>

      <h3>Advertising and sponsorships</h3>
      <p>
        We may run ordinary display advertising and clearly labelled
        sponsorships, including in the newsletter. Anything paid for is marked as
        such and is kept visually distinct from independent editorial content.
        Buying ad space or a sponsor slot gets you exactly that. It does not get
        a better review, a higher ranking, softened troubleshooting advice, or a
        guaranteed recommendation, and we will say so to anyone who asks for one.
      </p>

      <h3>Review units and loaned hardware</h3>
      <p>
        We accept free review products, manufacturer-supplied and loaned devices,
        and accessories provided for testing. Where a supplied or loaned product
        is central to an article or review, we say so on that page. Providing the
        hardware does not earn a review, favourable coverage, editorial approval
        rights, or a veto. If a device is disappointing, we will write that.
      </p>

      <h3>Commercial and referral partnerships</h3>
      <p>
        We may enter direct advertising deals, referral arrangements and other
        commercial partnerships with retailers, manufacturers and
        software makers. The same line applies to all of them: a partnership can
        fund the site, but it cannot decide what we conclude about a product.
      </p>

      <h2 id="boundaries">What we will not do</h2>
      <p>No amount of money buys any of the following:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A positive review, or guaranteed favourable coverage.</li>
        <li>A ranking, or the top spot in an independent comparison.</li>
        <li>A change to troubleshooting advice.</li>
        <li>A recommendation that is not disclosed as sponsored.</li>
        <li>
          Manufacturer control over an editorial conclusion, pre-approval of an
          independent review, or a veto over legitimate criticism.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="contact">Media, advertising and partnerships</h2>
      <p>
        Advertisers, manufacturers, retailers, PR and media contacts, and
        potential partners are welcome to get in touch:
        <a href="mailto:hello@readertweaks.com">hello@readertweaks.com</a>. We are
        happy to talk about ad placements, sponsorships, review units and
        partnerships.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not sell positive reviews, guaranteed verdicts, or placement in a
        ranking. If that is what you are after, we are not the right site.
      </p>
      <p>
        Corrections and reader suggestions go to the same address.
      </p>

      <h2 id="independence">Independence and ownership</h2>
      <p>
        ReaderTweaks is independently owned and not affiliated with Kobo, Amazon,
        Onyx, PocketBook, reMarkable or any other manufacturer. Product names are
        used for identification only. No manufacturer or advertiser has a stake
        in the site or a say in what it publishes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 720px; }
  .prose h1 { font-size: var(--step-3); }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 2.25rem; }
  .prose h3 { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
</style>
